{"code":200,"message":"OK","tweet":{"url":"https://x.com/matheorems/status/2109328633492111485","id":"2109328633492111485","text":"Gravitational time dilation: a clock held still at distance r from a mass runs at √(1 − r_s/r) of the rate of a clock far away, where r_s = 2GM/c² is the Schwarzschild radius.\n\nEach clock here is a light pulse bouncing between two mirrors, one round trip per second of its own time. The near clock is lowered down Flamm's paraboloid, the true shape of space around the mass, and its rate falls from 0.913 at 6 r_s to √(1 − 8/9) = 1/3 at r = 9/8 r_s, a hover just 12.5% outside a black hole's horizon. Then both restart together: in 15 s of far time the far clock ticks 15 times and the near clock 5. At the Earth's surface the same formula gives 1 − 7.0 × 10⁻¹⁰, about 60 µs a day.\n\nHow much time passes depends on where you are standing.","raw_text":{"text":"Gravitational time dilation: a clock held still at distance r from a mass runs at √(1 − r_s/r) of the rate of a clock far away, where r_s = 2GM/c² is the Schwarzschild radius.\n\nEach clock here is a light pulse bouncing between two mirrors, one round trip per second of its own time. The near clock is lowered down Flamm's paraboloid, the true shape of space around the mass, and its rate falls from 0.913 at 6 r_s to √(1 − 8/9) = 1/3 at r = 9/8 r_s, a hover just 12.5% outside a black hole's horizon. Then both restart together: in 15 s of far time the far clock ticks 15 times and the near clock 5. At the Earth's surface the same formula gives 1 − 7.0 × 10⁻¹⁰, about 60 µs a day.\n\nHow much time passes depends on where you are standing.","display_text_range":[0,738],"facets":[{"type":"media","indices":[277,300],"id":"2109328586461298688","display":"pic.x.com/20CX4WUBtN","original":"https://t.co/20CX4WUBtN","replacement":"https://x.com/matheorems/status/2109328633492111485/video/1"}]},"author":{"screen_name":"matheorems","url":"https://x.com/matheorems","id":"2077011768065593344","followers":8